  "query_preview": "..(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 352 PM EST WED NOV 4 2026\n\nThe extended period of the forecast begins on Friday, as an upper \nlevel low will be sinking southward from the Upper Great Lakes \nregion into the northern Ohio Valley throughout the day. The ECMWF \nshows some precipitation on the southern extent of this low, \nreaching northern Kentucky and possibly our far northern CWA. \nMeanwhile, the GFS keeps us more dry other than some very brief and \nlight rain potential in the evening. This run of the NBM included \nincreased cloud cover and some very slight pops across the northern \nCWA from 12 to 18Z, but nothing more. Will be interested to see how \nthe models, and therefore the NBM, trend with the next few runs - as \nit could increase the pops throughout the day, or could end up being \na dry day across our CWA. Either way, the impacts from any rain will \nbe minimal and should only affect the northern CWA. \n\nThe upper level low will then continue to track east-southeast \ntowards the New England coast through the weekend, placing Kentucky \nin the northerly flow portion of the system by Saturday. This, \ncoupled with strong high pressure and northerly flow at the surface, \nwill result in clearing skies and also a strong push of CAA. While \nthe current grids don't reflect it just yet, expect a decently \nstrong ridge/valley split in temperatures on both Saturday night and \nSunday night, with locations, especially deeper valleys, dropping \ninto the 20s. If this pans out, we will see our first hard freeze of \nthe season, effectively ending growing season.\n\nSurface high pressure and weak NW flow aloft will remain in place \nacross the region through the rest of the period, Monday through \nWednesday, bringing with it dry and sunny skies, along with light \nwinds. Temperatures will warm ever so slightly after Sunday, but \nshould generally remain in the 60s for highs and 30s for lows.",
